  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header" onclick="toggleSection(this)">
      <div class="section-num js-num">07</div>
      <div class="section-title">Asynchronous JS — Promises & Async/Await</div>
      <span class="section-arrow">›</span>
    </div>
    <div class="section-body">
      <pre><code><span class="cm">// Callbacks (old way — callback hell)</span>
<span class="fn">fetchData</span>(url, <span class="kw">function</span>(data) {
  <span class="fn">processData</span>(data, <span class="kw">function</span>(result) { <span class="cm">// nested...</span>
  });
});

<span class="cm">// Promise</span>
<span class="fn">fetch</span>(url)
  .<span class="fn">then</span>(res => res.<span class="fn">json</span>())
  .<span class="fn">then</span>(data => console.<span class="fn">log</span>(data))
  .<span class="fn">catch</span>(err => console.<span class="fn">error</span>(err))
  .<span class="fn">finally</span>(() => console.<span class="fn">log</span>(<span class="str">"done"</span>));

<span class="cm">// Async/Await (ES2017) — syntactic sugar over Promises</span>
<span class="kw">const</span> <span class="fn">getData</span> = <span class="kw">async</span> () => {
  <span class="kw">try</span> {
    <span class="kw">const</span> res = <span class="kw">await</span> <span class="fn">fetch</span>(url);
    <span class="kw">const</span> data = <span class="kw">await</span> res.<span class="fn">json</span>();
    console.<span class="fn">log</span>(data);
  } <span class="kw">catch</span> (err) {
    console.<span class="fn">error</span>(err);
  }
};

<span class="cm">// Promise.all — run in parallel</span>
<span class="kw">const</span> [a, b] = <span class="kw">await</span> Promise.<span class="fn">all</span>([<span class="fn">fetch</span>(url1), <span class="fn">fetch</span>(url2)]);</code></pre>
      <div class="tip"><strong>🎯 Interview tip:</strong> <code>Promise.all</code> — rejects if ANY promise fails. <code>Promise.allSettled</code> — waits for all, never rejects. <code>Promise.race</code> — resolves/rejects as soon as first one settles.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header" onclick="toggleSection(this)">
      <div class="section-num js-num">08</div>
      <div class="section-title">Closures, Hoisting & the Event Loop</div>
      <span class="section-arrow">›</span>
    </div>
    <div class="section-body">
      <h3>Closures</h3>
      <pre><code><span class="cm">// A function that remembers its outer scope</span>
<span class="kw">function</span> <span class="fn">counter</span>() {
  <span class="kw">let</span> count = <span class="num">0</span>;
  <span class="kw">return</span> () => ++count;  <span class="cm">// inner fn has closure over count</span>
}
<span class="kw">const</span> inc = <span class="fn">counter</span>();
<span class="fn">inc</span>(); <span class="cm">// 1</span>
<span class="fn">inc</span>(); <span class="cm">// 2</span></code></pre>
      <h3>Hoisting</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><code>var</code> declarations are hoisted to the top of their scope and initialized as <code>undefined</code></li>
        <li><code>let</code> and <code>const</code> are hoisted but NOT initialized — accessing them before declaration throws a <strong>ReferenceError</strong> (Temporal Dead Zone)</li>
        <li>Function <strong>declarations</strong> are fully hoisted (callable before definition)</li>
        <li>Function <strong>expressions</strong> are NOT hoisted</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Event Loop</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>JS is single-threaded — one task at a time on the <strong>Call Stack</strong></li>
        <li>Async tasks (timers, fetch) are offloaded to Web APIs</li>
        <li>When complete, callbacks are queued in the <strong>Callback Queue</strong> (macrotask) or <strong>Microtask Queue</strong> (Promises)</li>
        <li>The <strong>Event Loop</strong> checks: if Call Stack is empty → push from Microtask queue first, then Callback queue</li>
        <li>Microtasks (Promises) always run before macrotasks (setTimeout)</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="tip"><strong>🎯 Interview tip:</strong> Classic question — <code>setTimeout(() => console.log("A"), 0)</code> still runs after synchronous code and after Promise callbacks resolve.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-header" onclick="toggleSection(this)">
      <div class="section-num js-num">09</div>
      <div class="section-title">Local Storage, JSON & Modules</div>
      <span class="section-arrow">›</span>
    </div>
    <div class="section-body">
      <pre><code><span class="cm">// JSON</span>
JSON.<span class="fn">stringify</span>({ name: <span class="str">"Alice"</span> })   <span class="cm">// '{"name":"Alice"}'</span>
JSON.<span class="fn">parse</span>(<span class="str">'{"name":"Alice"}'</span>)        <span class="cm">// { name: "Alice" }</span>

<span class="cm">// localStorage — persists after tab close</span>
localStorage.<span class="fn">setItem</span>(<span class="str">"user"</span>, JSON.<span class="fn">stringify</span>(userData));
<span class="kw">const</span> user = JSON.<span class="fn">parse</span>(localStorage.<span class="fn">getItem</span>(<span class="str">"user"</span>));
localStorage.<span class="fn">removeItem</span>(<span class="str">"user"</span>);
localStorage.<span class="fn">clear</span>();

<span class="cm">// ES6 Modules</span>
<span class="cm">// math.js</span>
<span class="kw">export const</span> <span class="fn">add</span> = (a,b) => a+b;
<span class="kw">export default</span> <span class="kw">function</span> <span class="fn">multiply</span>(a,b) { <span class="kw">return</span> a*b; }

<span class="cm">// main.js</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> multiply, { add } <span class="kw">from</span> <span class="str">'./math.js'</span>;</code></pre>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

